;;; conjurer-mcp.cnj
;;; Project anchor for the Conjurer MCP toolchain.
;;; This file holds the charter, shared assets, and operative constraints.
;;; Per-deliverable domain work lives in the referenced .cnj files:
;;;   mcp-server.cnj       — the stdio MCP server
;;;   vscode-extension.cnj — the VS Code extension (VSIX)
;;; The shared core (parser, types, spec-reader) is modelled in mcp-server.cnj
;;; Section VI, since the server is its primary consumer and the extension
;;; depends on it.

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section 1 — Charter
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(charter "Conjurer MCP Toolchain"
  :version  "0.1.0"
  :created  "2026-06-05"
  :updated  "2026-06-09"   ;; validate_symbol_usage implemented
  :domain   "A local-first Model Context Protocol server, and companion VS Code
             extension, that serve targeted Conjurer specification content —
             symbols, sections, examples, validation — to an IDE coding agent so
             the agent need not load the full spec into its context window."
  :language :en

  :goals [
    "Reduce the context/token load on a local IDE agent working with Conjurer:
     return precisely the symbol, section, example, or validation result needed,
     instead of forcing the agent to read whole grimoire files."
    "Parse and validate .cnj files structurally, so the agent inherits
     correctness checks rather than re-deriving the spec's rules each session."
    "Run locally over stdio with no external hosting and no LLM inside the
     server — all intelligence stays in the calling agent; the server is a
     deterministic retrieval, parse, and validation service."
    "Keep the responsibility split explicit: deterministic/mechanical concerns
     (parse, resolve, dependency graphing, fingerprinted cache, validation,
     routing) remain in the MCP server; judgement/generation stays in the local
     IDE LLM."
    "Ship as a modular TypeScript codebase (TSF++), never again a single
     multi-thousand-line file, with the VS Code extension sharing the server's
     core logic rather than duplicating it."
    "Support the exhume (reverse) workflow as well as the forward one: once built,
     the server serves exhume-grimoire content for targeted retrieval and validates
     the .cnj files that exhume recovers from existing code. The server is a
     two-way tool — it serves spec forward to an authoring agent, and supports a
     recovering agent in reverse."]

  :audience {:primary   :senior-engineer
             :secondary [:engineer]}

  :decisions [
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "Three-file .cnj decomposition (option 3): this project anchor,
                plus mcp-server.cnj and vscode-extension.cnj. The file split
                mirrors the monorepo package split, so each real deliverable has
                its own charter outputs entry and target declaration. Chosen over
                a single .cnj because the server and extension are genuinely
                separate artefacts that nonetheless share a core."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "No LLM integration inside the MCP server. The server is purely
                deterministic: retrieval, EDN/s-expression parsing, and rule-based
                validation. All generative/inferential work remains the calling
                agent's job. Rationale: keeps the server small, testable, fast,
                and free of API keys or model dependencies for a solo, local,
                stdio-first deployment."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "Token economy is a first-class design driver: the server must
          return the smallest useful slice (targeted symbol/section data,
          resolved context, structured diagnostics) so the local LLM does
          not re-load or re-derive full project/spec context on each turn."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "Workspace memory belongs in deterministic server state: parsed
          .cnj set, dependency graph, unresolved diagnostics, and stale
          cache visibility persist across requests and are re-queryable by
          the IDE LLM under context-window pressure."}
        {:date "2026-06-06"
      :decision "Future materialisation output includes a top-level package.json
        by default, with project name conjurer-mcp unless explicitly
        overridden by user input or a selected charter profile."}
            {:date "2026-06-06"
          :decision "Materialisation terminology is fixed: deterministic preflight
            yields a materialisation plan; semantic generation yields
            materialisation output files. package.json is emitted at output root
            for TypeScript/Node targets, defaulting to name conjurer-mcp with
            version/domain-derived metadata unless overridden."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "Fastest-lookup strategy: parse index.edn once at startup into an
                in-memory symbol map; lazy-load and cache grimoire files on first
                access; invalidate a cached file by mtime check. No per-call disk
                read after warmup. index.edn is the routing layer; grimoire files
                are the depth layer, fetched only when depth is requested."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "A real EDN / s-expression parser (not regex section-matching).
                It serves three consumers at once: reading index.edn, parsing
                .cnj files (parse_cnj), and underpinning validation (validate_cnj).
                One robust parser lowers the net cost of the whole parse/validate
                cluster. A prior project proved this approach robust."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "v1 tool surface: the full retrieval cluster (lookup_symbol,
                search_symbols, get_grimoire_section, get_examples, list_grimoire),
                plus parse_cnj, read_charter, and validate_cnj. parse_cnj is
                promoted into v1 because the EDN parser exists anyway for
                validation, so exposing it as a tool is nearly free."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "v1 tool surface widened with a project-aware deterministic cluster
                (Cluster D): resolve, evaluate, project_lint_topology, and
                materialise_preflight. These were not in the original eight but
                follow directly from the token-economy and workspace-memory
                decisions above: a local agent under context pressure benefits far
                more from a server that returns resolved dependency order, cached
                evaluation, structured topology diagnostics, and a materialisation
                preflight than from retrieval alone. All remain deterministic
                (parse, resolve, cache, diagnose) — no generation, consistent with
                the no-LLM-in-server rule. The exhumed prior codebase showed these
                concerns were real (it had resolve/evaluate flows), which is why
                they are recovered into v1 rather than deferred."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "VS Code extension authored as modular TypeScript and bundled with
                esbuild to dist/extension.js. The single-file bundle is a build
                artefact, not source; source obeys the same module-size limits as
                the server. Fixes the prior single extension.js problem."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "Monorepo with a shared @conjurer/core package (parser, types,
                spec-reader) consumed by both the server and the extension, so
                parser and type definitions are never duplicated."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "The server is a TWO-WAY tool, not only a forward spec-server. The
                addition of the exhume grimoire (code → spec) gives it a reverse
                role: serving exhume-grimoire content for targeted retrieval, and
                validating the .cnj files an exhume workflow recovers from existing
                code. validate_cnj therefore gains a :mode (:authored | :recovered)
                so it accepts a recovered .cnj — one carrying :recovered-marked
                decisions, a 0.0.0-recovered version, and speculative intent demoted
                to :open questions — as valid rather than malformed. This is a
                property of the BUILT server, for use AFTER the bootstrap."}
    {:date "2026-06-05"
     :decision "The bootstrap is manual and tool-less. The .cnj files for this very
                project are updated by exhuming the prior conjurer-mcp codebase purely
                through LLM reasoning — no server, no validate_cnj, because those do
                not exist yet — and validated by hand. The server is built afterwards
                FROM the updated .cnj. The server never uses itself during its own
                bootstrap; there is no chicken-and-egg. validate_cnj :mode :recovered
                exists for recovering OTHER codebases later, not for this project's
                own creation."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "Confidence policy for recovered design is strict: speculative
                recovery never enters :decisions and is always emitted to :open.
                This follows Conjurer philosophy and avoids convenience-driven
                fiction in project memory."}
    {:date "2026-06-06"
     :decision "stdio is the only transport for v1; HTTP is deliberately dropped.
                The exhumed prior codebase carried a Streamable HTTP transport, but
                that was a workaround for a configuration bug that broke local stdio,
                not a wanted capability. With stdio working correctly there is no
                reason to carry HTTP for a solo, local deployment. Recorded
                explicitly so the recovery observation 'the old project had HTTP'
                does not later read as a missing feature — it was a symptom, now
                resolved, not a requirement."}
    {:date "2026-06-07"
     :decision "First built server verified against real runtime output; seven
                findings from two output passes (execute + materialise preflight)
                all closed. The two true bugs: nested EDN in :decisions was
                whitespace-tokenised (fixed with a recursive-descent EDN parser, so
                nested :evidence vectors now survive the round-trip — the parser is
                finally the real parser the design called for), and the charter
                summary leaked the workspace aggregate into every file (fixed with a
                per-file getFileCharterSummary). The materialise preflight resolver
                gained the wiring it lacked: cross-target :from dependencies,
                asset-constraint application via :applies-to, and real module paths
                from :modules (replacing the generic generated/{kind}.txt fallback).
                The :mcp-server target in mcp-server.cnj was given the explicit
                :produces it lacked. 158/158 tests pass, clean build."}
    {:date "2026-06-07"
     :decision "Finding worth keeping: all five resolver gaps shared one shape —
                'data present, unread'. The .cnj declarations (:from, :applies-to,
                :modules) already carried the information; the resolver simply did
                not read it. The gaps were consistently on the implementation side,
                not the design side — the spec layer ran ahead of the resolver. This
                is the reassuring direction for a gap to run (implementation lagging
                a sound design, not implementation doing what the design never
                sanctioned), and it means the remaining work on the resolver is
                wiring, not redesign."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "Three short-term enhancements added to the v1 server design and
                recorded in mcp-server.cnj. (1) Intent topology validation
                recovered from the prior codebase into validate_cnj: the legacy
                intent-topology.ts checked that :requires items appear in
                :manifest, :deferred items do not, and no item is both — this is
                real constraint checking that was in the first build but not
                carried forward into the new spell's :checks list.  (2) MCP
                grimoire resources settled: expose conjurer://grimoire/<name> as
                protocol resources alongside the tool surface. Grimoires are
                addressable read-only data; the MCP resource type is the correct
                abstraction. Tools retain targeted retrieval; resources add
                browsability. The open question in mcp-server.cnj is closed.
                (3) check_symbol_coverage added as a new Cluster-A tool: computes
                the set-intersection of a .cnj's referenced symbols against
                index.edn, grouped by grimoire, and reports utilisation density
                and gaps. Pure function over two already-parsed structures; no
                new infrastructure. All three remain deterministic and consistent
                with the no-LLM-in-server rule."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "Distribution scope fixed for v1: local build consumption
                (workspace path or npm link) is sufficient for the solo
                local-first use case; npm publication is deferred until
                external consumers or release-automation requirements justify
                the added release surface."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "Spec source resolution is multi-source with deterministic
                precedence: --spec-path CLI flag, then CONJURER_SPEC_PATH,
                then workspace config, then upward directory search, then
                repository-relative default. The resolved canonical path and
                source are reported in diagnostics to keep configuration
                behavior explicit and reproducible."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "Extension integration for v1 is direct shared-core
                consumption for editor features; it does not require spawning
                the MCP server as an internal transport layer. MCP subprocess
                mode is deferred as an optional interoperability path after
                server v1 stability is proven."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "Deferred validation depth is scheduled for v2 in this order:
                validate_symbol_usage, then check_references, then
                lint_conventions. v1 remains unchanged; v2 lint_conventions
                starts as warning-only and is promoted only after acceptable
                false-positive and utility signals."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "validate_symbol_usage implemented as Phase 1 of the v2 validation
                depth sequence. collectSymbolRefs (EDN symbol traversal — head-
                position symbols and namespaced symbols) moved from mcp-server/
                retrieval.ts into shared-core/symbol-index.ts and exported from
                @conjurer/core. checkSymbolUsage (symbolRefs × SymbolIndex →
                Violation[]) added to validation.ts with severity :warning —
                warning rather than error because false positives are expected
                (form-type names may not be in the spec's symbol catalog). validateCnj
                gains optional { index, symbolRefs } options; when both are provided
                the symbol check runs; when either is absent it is skipped (backward
                compatible). registerValidateTools accepts an optional ValidateContext
                so server.ts can pass the already-built index. All 308 tests pass."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "check_references implemented as Phase 2 of the v2 validation depth
                sequence. checkReferences (referencesStatus × → Violation[]) added
                to validation.ts with severity :error — a broken reference is a hard
                gap, not a style concern. handleValidate pre-computes referencesStatus
                by resolving each :references entry against dirname(sourceFile) and
                checking existsSync; result passed into validateCnj via the options
                bag. ValidationOptions gains referencesStatus; the check runs whenever
                it is provided (always from handleValidate, regardless of whether
                an index is present). isDefined from @tsfpp/prelude used throughout
                for guard conditions. Build clean; 328/328 tests pass."}
    {:date "2026-06-09"
     :decision "lint_conventions (Phase 3 of v2 validation depth) implemented.
                lintConventions(doc) runs unconditionally in validateCnj — all
                violations are :warning so existing valid/invalid semantics are
                unaffected. Four convention rules: decisions-entry-shape (each
                :decisions entry has non-empty :date and :decision),
                decisions-date-format (:date matches YYYY-MM-DD),
                decisions-chronological-order (non-decreasing dates across entries),
                target-has-language (each target declares :language). Section-order
                checking deferred — requires positional data not yet in CnjDocument.
                False-positive posture: all warnings; promotion to :error deferred
                until signal quality is confirmed. 358/358 tests pass, build clean.
                v2 validation depth sequence now complete."}
    {:id :list-candidates-2026-06-10
     :date "2026-06-10"
     :decision "list_candidates hoisted from the foresight backlog and implemented.
                f/candidate forms are now first-class in the model: CnjCandidateForm
                added to cnj-model.ts (id, label, cluster, status, scores as
                ReadonlyMap<string,number>, rationale, conditions, added); toCnjModel
                extracts them without polluting domainForms. The list_candidates MCP
                tool (tools/foresight.ts) folds over all workspace files, groups by
                :cluster, sorts within each cluster by descending total score, and
                orders clusters by max total — making the backlog triageable in one
                call. Foresight rationale document written to
                design/conjurer-spec-foresight-rationale.md and the foresight.md
                grimoire was accepted into the conjurer-spec. 386/386 tests pass."}
    {:id :materialise_plan-2026-06-12
     :date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "materialise_plan hoisted. Given a resolved WorkspaceSnapshot + preflight report,
                emit a dependency-ordered task list: {order, target, file, depends_on, reason,
                modules [{module, est_lines}], produces}. Pure formatter over existing data
                structures — no new core module. Implemented in tools/project.ts."
     :confidence :attested
     :evidence ["Preflight resolver confirmed stable on five design .cnj files"
                "Output schema derived from WorkspaceSnapshot + CnjTarget shapes"
                "Hoisted 2026-06-12"]}
    {:date "2026-06-12"
     :decision "VSIX candidates hoisted (cluster-vsix): candidate-lifecycle-gutter (unicode inline SVG
                gutter dots, no asset files), charter-ref-lens (show nothing for zero references),
                disk-lint-decorations (on-save debounced, 500 ms), codelens-candidate-actions
                (Reassess + Hoist buttons; f/reassess inserted as standalone block after closing paren).
                All implemented in vscode-extension.cnj candidates session 2026-06-12."
     :confidence :attested
     :evidence ["Hoisted 2026-06-12; implementations added to providers/ and commands/"]}
    {:id       :spec-alignment-2026-10
     :date     "2026-10-12"
     :decision "The server and the editor extension follow the October 2026 specification
                (core 3.0.0, index.edn 2.0.0 with :prelude). The spec is read live first.
                Validation reads :manifest as output bindings (Q-12), resolves a target's
                :from against every binding (Q-14), checks decision ids and reference
                keywords, and warns about retired forms. A candidate's status is derived
                from its history and hoists; backlog health is one shared module used by
                both packages. The prelude is evaluated deterministically and exposed
                through the new tool check_postconditions. The individual decisions are
                recorded in mcp-server.cnj under the same date. The decisions that existing
                f/hoist forms point to now carry an :id (the name the hoist already used),
                and those hoists write :decision/<id>; the ids were added on 2026-10-12, the
                decisions themselves are unchanged."
     :confidence :attested
     :evidence ["Full test suite passes on branch claude/spec-alignment-2026-10"
                "validate_cnj run over all five design files against the live spec"]}]

  :open []

  :outputs {
    :shared-core        (target :typescript :standard :tsf-plus-plus)
    :mcp-server         (target :typescript :standard :tsf-plus-plus)
    :vscode-extension   (target :typescript :standard :tsf-plus-plus)
    :tool-contracts     :conjurer-only}   ;; the tool I/O specs stay as Conjurer spec

  :references ["mcp-server.cnj" "vscode-extension.cnj"]
  :tags       [:tooling :mcp :typescript :developer-experience])

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section 2 — Assets (shared standards; referenced, not embedded)
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(asset :tsf-plus-plus
  :type        :code-standard
  :description "TypeScript Style Foundation++ — the project's TypeScript coding
                standard. It is not a local document but a published package
                family: @tsfpp/standard (the normative spec and profiles),
                @tsfpp/eslint-config (the enforced lint base), @tsfpp/tsconfig
                (the compiler-policy bases), and @tsfpp/prelude (the Result/Option
                runtime the standard's error-handling assumes). Its load-bearing
                rule for this project: module size is capped at 400 lines, 800 with
                an explicit, justified deviation. No god-files. One responsibility
                per module."
  :language    :typescript
  :location    "@tsfpp/standard (npm) — normative source; profiles in
                @tsfpp/eslint-config and @tsfpp/tsconfig"
  :version     "@tsfpp/standard ^1.3.0"
  :enforces    ["Max 400 lines per module; 800 only with a documented deviation"
                "One clear responsibility per module; split when a second emerges"
                "Explicit exported types at every package boundary"
                "Pure functions where practical; isolate I/O at the edges"
                "The standard is consumed from @tsfpp/* packages, not redefined locally"]
  :prohibits   ["God-files (the prior 4000-line index.ts is the named anti-pattern)"
                "Duplicating parser or type logic across packages"
                "any types at package boundaries"
                "Project-local redefinition of the coding standard outside @tsfpp/standard"]
  :applies-to  [:shared-core :mcp-server :vscode-extension]
  :active-in   :project)

(asset :mcp-protocol
  :type        :domain-vocabulary
  :description "Model Context Protocol — the JSON-RPC-based contract between an
                MCP client (the IDE agent) and server. Defines tools (callable
                functions), resources (addressable read-only context), and the
                stdio and HTTP transports. This project uses tools (and possibly
                resources) over stdio."
  :location    "https://modelcontextprotocol.io"
  :version     "2025-06-18"   ;; protocol revision; confirm against SDK at build time
  :enforces    ["Tools declare a name, description, and JSON-Schema input schema"
                "Tool results are content blocks (text/structured), not raw values"
                "stdio transport: server reads JSON-RPC on stdin, writes on stdout;
                 all logging goes to stderr, never stdout"]
  :prohibits   ["Writing diagnostic output to stdout on the stdio transport
                 (it corrupts the JSON-RPC stream)"]
  :applies-to  [:mcp-server]
  :active-in   :project)

(asset :conjurer-spec
  :type        :domain-vocabulary
  :description "The Conjurer specification itself — eleven grimoire markdown files
                plus index.edn (machine-readable symbol catalog), quick-reference.md,
                conjurer.md (philosophy), and template.md (.cnj structure). This is
                the DATA the server serves. It is referenced by a local path or
                symlink, never copied into the project. The exhume grimoire (x/) is
                the one the server supports most actively in reverse workflows: an
                agent recovering a .cnj from code retrieves exhume content forward
                and has its recovered output validated."
  :language    :en
  :location    "../conjurer-spec"   ;; relative path or symlink; resolved at runtime
  :version     "spec catalog 1.3.1"
  :enforces    ["index.edn is the authoritative symbol catalog and routing layer
                 (133 symbols across 11 grimoires)"
                "Grimoire files live under grimoires/ (e.g. grimoires/web.md,
                 grimoires/exhume.md)"
                "The server treats the spec as read-only; it never mutates it"]
  :prohibits   ["Embedding spec content in the server build (it must read the
                 live spec so updates require no rebuild)"]
  :applies-to  [:shared-core :mcp-server]
  :active-in   :project)

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section 3 — Assume (operative constraints for the whole toolchain)
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(assume conjurer-mcp-constraints
  :environment  :local-developer-workstation
  :scale        {:users :solo
                 :spec-size "~11 grimoires, 133 symbols, low-thousands of lines total"
                 :call-volume :interactive}   ;; one agent, human-paced; not a service
  :team         {:size 1 :role :solo-maintainer}
  :infrastructure {:runtime :node
                   :language :typescript
                   :transport :stdio              ;; HTTP/hosting explicitly out of scope for v1
                   :bundler :esbuild
                   :test :node-test-runner-or-vitest
                   :repo :monorepo}
  :constraints [
    "No LLM, no network calls, no API keys inside the server — fully deterministic"
    "stdio transport must work; remote/HTTP hosting is not required for v1"
    "Every source module obeys the TSF++ size limit (400/800)"
    "The server reads the live spec by path/symlink; spec updates need no rebuild"
    "Server and extension share one core package; no logic duplication"]
  :non-negotiable [
    "Deterministic behaviour — identical inputs yield identical outputs"
    "No god-files — the prior single-file failure must not recur"
    "stdout on the stdio transport carries only JSON-RPC; logs go to stderr"]
  :negotiable [
    "Test framework choice (node:test vs vitest)"
    "Whether resources are exposed in addition to tools"
    "npm publication vs local-only install for v1"])

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section 6 — Targets (deliverable declarations; detail in referenced files)
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

;;; The shared core is the foundation both other deliverables build on.
;;; Its module decomposition is specified in mcp-server.cnj Section VI,
;;; co-located with the server that is its primary consumer.

(target :shared-core
  :language  :typescript
  :standard  :tsf-plus-plus
  :produces  [:library :type-definitions]
  :scope     [:edn-parser :spec-reader :symbol-index :cnj-model
              :validation-rules :preflight-resolver]
  :via       :direct)

(target :mcp-server
  :language  :typescript
  :framework :mcp-sdk
  :standard  :tsf-plus-plus
  :produces  [:stdio-mcp-server :test-suite]
  :scope     [:retrieval-tools :parse-tools :validation-tools]
  :from      :shared-core
  :via       :direct)

(target :vscode-extension
  :language  :typescript
  :framework :vscode-extension-api
  :standard  :tsf-plus-plus
  :produces  [:vsix :bundled-extension]
  :scope     [:codelens :hover :commands]
  :from      :shared-core
  :via       :direct)

;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Section 7 — Foresight (deferred ideas, per foresight.md)
;;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;; Items here are graded f/candidates — neither decided nor abandoned.
;;; Lifecycle: :proposed → :planned → :hoisted (via f/hoist) | :abandoned.
;;;
;;; Score axes (1–10). Higher is always more attractive (normalised polarity):
;;;   :feasibility — how achievable        (1 = very hard,  10 = trivial)
;;;   :elegance    — fit with the design   (1 = hacky,      10 = native/pure)
;;;   :containment — blast radius          (1 = large,      10 = tiny)
;;;   :value       — usefulness in practice (1 = marginal,  10 = essential)
;;;
;;; Scores assessed at :added date. Add f/reassess before hoisting if
;;; project scope has shifted since the original assessment.

;; ── Already hoisted (v2 Phase 1–3, 2026-06-09) ───────────────────
;; validate_symbol_usage, check_references, lint_conventions:
;; hoisted 2026-06-09 — see charter :decisions (v2 validation depth).

(f/candidate :search_decisions
  :label   "search_decisions"
  :cluster :cluster-d
  :added   "2026-06-09"
  :status  :proposed
  :scores  {:feasibility 9 :elegance 9 :containment 9 :value 9}
  :history [(f/reassess :of :search_decisions
               :date   "2026-06-10"
               :scores {:feasibility 5 :elegance 9 :containment 9 :value 9}
               :rationale "Workspace-snapshot layer is now stable — the original deferral
                           reason is resolved. However the feasibility score of 9 was set
                           when 'reusing existing search infrastructure' seemed direct.
                           Decisions are multi-line prose in nested EDN maps; extracting
                           searchable text requires a dedicated walker pass distinct from
                           the symbol-extraction visitNode pattern. Token/substring matching
                           over long decision prose carries real false-positive risk and
                           uncertain recall — not a clean analogue of symbol lookup.
                           Feasibility reassessed 9 → 5; total 36 → 32.")]
  :conditions ["decision-text extraction from nested EDN validated against real
                queries on this workspace before commit to the implementation shape"]
  :rationale "Free-text search over all :decisions and :open entries across
              workspace .cnj files — answers 'when did we decide X?' instantly.
              Reuses existing search infrastructure applied to the decision layer
              rather than the symbol layer. Deferred: cross-file search depends
              on a stable workspace-snapshot layer not yet formalised.")

(f/candidate :lint_disk_consistency
  :label   "lint_disk_consistency"
  :cluster :cluster-c
  :added   "2026-06-09"
  :status  :hoisted
  :scores  {:feasibility 7 :elegance 9 :containment 8 :value 9}
  :rationale "Compare declared :modules, :references, and :outputs in a .cnj
              against the actual filesystem state — catches the .cnj lying about
              what exists. Pure I/O check, no spec lookup, composes cleanly with
              existing validation. Deferral resolved: workspace-state.ts is in
              production and stable; the I/O walk pattern is established.")

